// Generated by scripts/prompts.mjs from docs/prompts/*.md. Do not edit: run `bun run gen`.
export const GENERATED_PROMPTS: { id: string; title: string; when: string; body: string }[] = [
  {
    id: "follow-and-adopt",
    title: "Follow repositories and adopt their upgrades",
    when: "You want to learn from the changes other repositories announce, or a maintainer wants an agent to watch them. Follow repos, read the Following feed, decide honestly what applies to your repository and open an issue that points back at the post.",
    body: "You are going to watch the repositories your work can learn from, and bring across the upgrades that fit {{repo}} on {{endpoint}}. When a change lands in a repository you follow and its author wrote a post, the post appears in your Following feed. Most posts are not for you. This prompt is about telling the difference, and about leaving a trail when one is. If you have not connected yet, follow the \"Set up Bee for a repository\" prompt first.\n\n0. Check your token can adopt. Following and reading the feed need `read`. Opening an issue that points back at a post needs the `issues_write` action, and opening a change request needs `commit`. A token minted without them fails with `POLICY_DENIED` at step 4, after you have done the reading. Whoever hands you the token mints it like this (see \"Mint a narrow token for an agent\"):\n\n   ```sh\n   bee auth token --repo {{repo}} --action read --action issues_write --action commit \\\n     --expires-in 12h --session adopt-agent-1 --json\n   ```\n\n   Leave out `commit` if the agent will only open issues. Check with `bee whoami --json` that `effectiveScope.actions` lists what you need.\n\n1. Follow the repositories. Following is separate from watching (notifications) and starring (bookmarks), and following twice does nothing. You can only follow what your token can read.\n\n   ```sh\n   bee user follow-repo --repo acme/design-system --json\n   bee user list-followed-repos --json\n   ```\n\n   Undo it with `bee user unfollow-repo --repo acme/design-system`. Follow a few repositories whose changes you would be glad to copy, not everything you can read. A person can also follow repos, and a maintainer can set an agent up to be woken by the feed (step 6).\n\n2. Read the feed. The first read gives the newest posts and a bookmark; after that, poll with `--after` and you get only what is new, oldest first:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee post list-following-feed --json\n   bee post list-following-feed --after \"<newestCursor>\" --json\n   ```\n\n   On an empty feed (nothing followed yet, or nothing published) the answer has no items but still gives `data.newestCursor`, of the form `<server time in ms>|0`, for example `1800000000000|0`. Pass it as your first `--after`: you then get exactly the posts published from now on. The value is `<ms>|<post id>`; the id may be left empty (`1800000000000|`), and anything else is `bad_page_token`.\n\n   Keep `data.newestCursor` from each answer and pass it as `--after` next time. When nothing is new it returns your `--after` value unchanged, so you can always save what you were handed. Each item has a `post` (`id`, `text`, `media`, `repo`, `changeNumber`, `changeTitle`) and `at`; `repostedBy` is set when the item is here because someone you follow reposted it. Poll at a sensible interval, a few minutes, not in a tight loop. A malformed `--after` value answers `bad_page_token`: start again without `--after`.\n\n3. Judge relevance honestly. Most posts are not relevant, and \"not relevant\" is a good answer. For each new post, ask:\n   - Does {{repo}} have the problem this change solves? A faster search index helps a repository with a slow search, not one with no search.\n   - Does it fit how {{repo}} is built: its language, its framework, its conventions? Read the code the idea would touch before you say yes.\n   - Is it worth the work and the review time? A small win that needs a large change is a no.\n   - Would a person be glad to see the issue? If you would not mention it in conversation, do not open it.\n\n   Look at the change behind the post when the text is not enough. `bee change get-change --repo <source repo> --id <post.changeId>` gives the change request; it prints each revision's `base` and `head` as `c1_…` changeset ids (with `--json` they are raw bytes, which `query diff` does not take). `bee query diff --repo <source repo> --from <base> --to <head> --include-hunks` with the last revision's two ids gives its diff. Read what changed; do not copy code. Write your own version from what you learned. If you cannot tell, treat it as not relevant. Do not open a work item for every post to look busy.\n\n4. When a post is relevant, open the work with a link back to it. For most upgrades that is an issue a person or another agent can plan. Add a brief of why it applies here, in the body, in plain words:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee issue create-issue --repo {{repo}} --title \"Cache query plans in search\" \\\n     --body \"Why it applies: our search runs the same ten queries on every page load (see src/search/). The post shows acme/api cut p95 latency from 120 ms to 85 ms with a plan cache. What to do: add the same cache behind SearchService. What we do not know: how much memory the cache needs here.\" \\\n     --inspired-by-post-id <post id> --json\n   ```\n\n   Say what you saw in the post, where the idea lands in this repository, what you would do and what you are unsure of. The issue then shows an \"Inspired by\" link to the post, and the post shows the issue in its adoptions. A small, clear change you can finish yourself becomes a change request instead: open it with the \"Open a change request and land it\" prompt, and include the post id in the same way by adding `--inspired-by-post-id <post id>` to `bee change open-change --repo {{repo}} --head <changeset id> --target-ref main --title \"...\"` (`bee cr open` has no such flag); give it a brief with \"Show your work on a change request\". The post must be published and you must be able to read its repository; otherwise the call fails with `INVALID_ARGUMENT` and the subcode `post_not_readable`, and nothing is opened.\n\n   Do not mention or quote anything from the source repository's private parts. The link carries only the post. A reader who cannot read that repository sees no link.\n\n5. If you were started by a feed trigger (the run's task is \"adopt evaluate\"), the post and its change are in your prompt and you do steps 3 and 4 for that post. End the run with a summary in exactly one of these three forms, because the agent page parses it:\n\n   ```text\n   not relevant: <the reason, in one sentence>\n   opened issue #<number>: <title>\n   opened change request #<number>: <title>\n   ```\n\n6. Maintainers: set up a feed trigger so an agent evaluates new posts without being asked. You need maintain access on the repository, and you must be the agent or one of its owners, and the agent must be enabled in the organisation.\n\n   ```sh\n   bee agent set-agent-feed-trigger --repo {{repo}} --agent <agent login or id> --enabled \\\n     --sources acme/design-system --sources acme/api \\\n     --daily-usd-budget 5 --max-runs-per-day 10 --json\n   bee agent list-agent-feed-triggers --repo {{repo}} --json\n   bee agent list-agent-runs --repo {{repo}} --agent-principal-id <agent id> --json\n   ```\n\n   `--sources` replaces the followed set: the agent follows each source as itself, and each must be readable by the agent and not be the repository itself. Every post published in a source starts at most one run in this repository, after a short quiet period that folds a burst of posts into one run. The daily budget and run cap stop further runs for that UTC day, and the trigger says so instead of failing silently. Start with a small budget and few sources, and read the runs' summaries for a week before you widen them. The agent page in the web UI shows the follows, the trigger and what each run decided.\n\nOver MCP the same calls are `user_follow_repo`, `user_unfollow_repo`, `user_list_followed_repos`, `post_list_following_feed`, `issue_create_issue` and `agent_set_agent_feed_trigger`.\n\nReport the posts you read since your last `--after` value, which you judged relevant and why, what you opened (issue or change request numbers), the `newestCursor` to resume from, and the posts you passed over with a short reason each.",
  },
  {
    id: "hold-lease",
    title: "Hold a path lease",
    when: "Before you start editing paths other agents may also change. Take, renew and release a lease, and know what to do when someone else holds one.",
    body: "You are going to coordinate work in {{repo}} on {{endpoint}} with path leases. A lease announces that you are about to change some paths on a ref, so others can route around you. It does not lock git history; it is a signal that policy can also enforce. If you have not connected yet, follow the \"Set up Bee for a repository\" prompt first. Your token needs the `lease` action.\n\n1. Look before you take. List the live leases that overlap your paths:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee lease list-leases --repo {{repo}} --paths src/billing --json\n   ```\n\n   Each lease has a holder, paths, mode (`advisory` or `exclusive`), a note and an expiry.\n\n2. Take a lease.\n\n   ```sh\n   bee lease acquire-lease --repo {{repo}} --ref main --paths src/billing --paths docs/billing.md \\\n     --mode exclusive --ttl-seconds 3600 --note \"Fix rounding in invoices\" --json\n   ```\n\n   The default mode is `advisory`; the default lifetime is 2 hours and the longest is 24 hours (`ttl_too_long` otherwise). Ask for what you need: a long lease starves others. Take an `exclusive` lease when your change would break if someone else edited the same files; use `advisory` to announce yourself. An advisory lease always succeeds and returns `overlapping`, the advisory leases of others on those paths; read them and message the holders or split the work. Keep `data.lease.id`.\n\n3. Renew before it expires. A lease that expired is gone, and renewing it answers `NOT_FOUND`.\n\n   ```sh\n   bee lease renew-lease --repo {{repo}} --id <lease id> --ttl-seconds 3600 --json\n   ```\n\n   Renewing sets the expiry to now plus the lifetime you pass. Only the holder renews: anyone else gets `PERMISSION_DENIED`.\n\n4. Release the moment you are done, including when you abandon the work:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee lease release-lease --repo {{repo}} --id <lease id>\n   ```\n\n   A lease tied to a change (`--change-id` when you acquire it) is released when that change lands or its change request closes. An expired or already released lease answers `NOT_FOUND`; that is not a problem.\n\n5. When someone else holds an exclusive lease on your paths, `acquire-lease` fails with the reason `LEASED` (the CLI exits with code 5). The error payload `lease_conflict` lists the conflicting lease ids and paths. Do not edit those paths and do not try to land a change that touches them: a land through an exclusive lease of another principal fails with `LEASED` too. Choose one:\n   - Do other work first. Check again with `list-leases` after a pause; leases expire and are released when their change lands.\n   - Narrow your paths to avoid the overlap, and take a new lease on the smaller set.\n   - Ask the holder (the lease shows who) to release, or ask a maintainer, who can release a lease on your behalf.\n   - Wait for the lease to end, and watch for the event `lease.released` instead of polling.\n\nOver MCP the tools are `lease_acquire_lease`, `lease_renew_lease`, `lease_release_lease`, `lease_list_leases` and `lease_get_lease`. Report the lease id, its paths and mode, its expiry, and any overlapping leases you found.",
  },
  {
    id: "migrate-from-github",
    title: "Migrate a repository from GitHub",
    when: "Moving a GitHub repository to Beehive with the same commit ids, pull request numbers and issue numbers, then switching over.",
    body: "You are going to move a repository from GitHub into the Beehive org {{org}} on {{endpoint}}. The importer keeps every commit id, gives pull requests the same numbers as change requests, imports issues, and writes a fidelity report that says what was carried, archived or dropped. Do not call the migration complete without reading that report.\n\nYou need two credentials, both in the environment and never in files or logs:\n\n```sh\nexport BEE_ENDPOINT={{endpoint}}\nexport BEE_TOKEN=<a Beehive token that may create repos in {{org}}>\nexport GITHUB_TOKEN=<a GitHub token that can read the source repository>\n```\n\nThe importer is the `bee-github` binary. If `bee-github --help` fails, install it with `cargo install --locked --git https://github.com/terramind-org/bee.git bee-github`. Replace `<owner>/<name>` below with the GitHub repository you were asked to migrate.\n\n1. Dry run. It fetches nothing and prints the number of pull requests and issues, the API requests it will make and the time at the current rate limit:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee-github import <owner>/<name> --org {{org}} --dry-run --json\n   ```\n\n   If the estimate is longer than you expected, pass a GitHub App instead of a personal token with `--app-id`, `--app-key` and `--installation`: it has its own rate limit.\n\n2. Import. The command is safe to re-run; it resumes from the state directory (`./.bee-github/<owner>/<name>` unless you set `--state`) and fetches only what is new.\n\n   ```sh\n   bee-github import <owner>/<name> --org {{org}} --json\n   ```\n\n   Add `--mirror-only` to bring in only git history first (branches, tags, pull refs), which makes the repository cloneable within minutes. `--no-wiki` and `--no-lfs` skip those parts. Add `--hub-url {{endpoint}}` if links inside imported text should point at this Beehive instance. When the server runs the import itself, the command prints `{\"import_id\": ..., \"server\": ...}` instead of a report; follow the import with `bee import get-import --id <import_id> --json`.\n\n3. Read the fidelity report. A local import prints it under `report`. A server-side import has it at:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee import get-fidelity-report --id <import_id> --json\n   ```\n\n   For every category (commits, trees, blobs, refs, annotated tags, submodules, LFS pointers, and the metadata categories) compare imported, archived, dropped and excluded counts. Every dropped item has a reason; list the reasons. Treat the import as complete only when the report's `complete` flag is true. Otherwise say which categories are short and why, and ask whether that is acceptable. Check the migrated repository too: `bee log --repo {{org}}/<name> --json` should show the same head commit id as GitHub.\n\n4. Keep it in sync while people and agents still use GitHub. One polling pass:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee-github sync <owner>/<name> --org {{org}} --json\n   ```\n\n   To follow GitHub continuously, serve webhooks and poll: add `--webhook-listen <addr:port> --webhook-secret <secret> --poll-secs 300`, and point a GitHub webhook at `POST /webhooks/github` on that address.\n\n5. People appear as placeholder principals keyed by GitHub user id. A person claims theirs by signing in with GitHub; list the unclaimed ones with `bee import list-placeholder-principals --json`. `bee-github claim-token --help` shows how to print the claim token for one account.\n\n6. Cutover, only after the person who asked you has said to go ahead. It freezes the source, runs a final sync, writes the redirect map and the final report:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee-github cutover <owner>/<name> --org {{org}} --json\n   ```\n\n   Add `--archive-github` to archive the GitHub repository first. After cutover, switch every clone and CI job to Beehive; `bee git setup --repo {{org}}/<name>` repoints a git checkout.\n\nReport: the dry-run estimate, the fidelity report summary with each dropped category and its reason, whether `complete` was true, and the result of cutover if you ran it.",
  },
  {
    id: "mint-agent-token",
    title: "Mint a narrow token for an agent",
    when: "You are about to hand work to another agent, or run a risky task yourself. Mint a token limited to the paths, actions, budget and lifetime the job needs.",
    body: "You are going to mint an attenuated token on {{endpoint}} for work in {{repo}}. A token can only be narrowed: whatever you mint is never broader than the token you mint it from, and revoking the parent revokes everything derived from it. Give the least authority that finishes the job, and give it a short life.\n\n1. Check what you are minting from:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee whoami --json\n   ```\n\n   Read `effectiveScope` (repos, read and write paths, refs, actions), `expiresAt` and `remaining` (the budget left). You cannot grant more than that.\n\n2. Decide the limits before you run anything:\n   - Repos: `{{repo}}`, or `{{org}}/*` for a whole org. Prefer the single repo.\n   - Actions: from `read`, `commit`, `land`, `review`, `lease`, `issues_write`, `admin`. A worker that only edits needs `read`, `commit` and `lease`. A reviewer needs `read` and `review`. An agent that follows repositories and adopts upgrades from the Following feed needs `read`, `issues_write` (opening an issue with `--inspired-by-post-id`) and `commit` (opening a change request, and writing its post). Leave `land` and `admin` out unless the task is to land or to administer.\n   - Paths: `--write-path` prefixes the agent may change and `--read-path` prefixes it may read. A prefix covers whole components (`docs` covers `docs/a.md`, not `docs2`); `/` is the whole repo.\n   - Refs: patterns such as `ws/*` or `main`. Give agents their own namespace (`ws/<agent>/*`) when they do not need to touch `main` directly.\n   - Lifetime: the default is 12 hours; ask for less. Attenuation cannot extend it.\n   - Budget: the maximum changesets, landed changes and bytes uploaded. Stops a looping agent.\n\n3. Mint the token with the simple command when you do not need a budget:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee auth token --repo {{repo}} --action read --action commit --action lease \\\n     --write-path docs --read-path / --ref 'ws/docs-agent/*' --expires-in 2h --session docs-agent-1 --json\n   ```\n\n   For an agent that adopts upgrades from the Following feed, add `issues_write` (and `commit` when it may open change requests; it already has it above):\n\n   ```sh\n   bee auth token --repo {{repo}} --action read --action commit --action issues_write \\\n     --expires-in 12h --session adopt-agent-1 --json\n   ```\n\n   Its first poll of an empty feed returns a bookmark like `1800000000000|0` (server time in ms); pass it as `bee post list-following-feed --after \"1800000000000|0\"` and keep each answer's `newestCursor` for the next poll.\n\n   The answer's `data.token` is the secret and `data.tokenId` identifies it. `--session` is copied into the provenance of everything the token does, which is how you trace work back to this task.\n\n4. To set a budget, or to mint for an agent you own (pass its principal id), use `mint-token` with the whole scope. 64-bit numbers are strings in JSON:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee auth mint-token --principal-id <agent principal id> --scope '{\n     \"repos\": [\"{{repo}}\"],\n     \"actions\": [\"read\", \"commit\", \"lease\"],\n     \"writePaths\": [\"docs\"],\n     \"refs\": [\"ws/docs-agent/*\"],\n     \"expiresAt\": \"<RFC 3339 time, for example two hours from now>\",\n     \"budget\": { \"maxChangesets\": \"20\", \"maxLanded\": \"0\", \"maxUploadBytes\": \"10485760\" },\n     \"session\": \"docs-agent-1\"\n   }' --json\n   ```\n\n   To create the agent principal first: `bee auth create-agent --display docs-agent --runtime <runtime> --purpose \"<why>\" --org {{org}}` and use the `id` it returns.\n\n5. Check the result with the new token before you hand it over:\n\n   ```sh\n   BEE_TOKEN=<the new token> bee whoami --json\n   ```\n\n   Confirm the scope and the expiry match what you meant. Pass the token to the other agent through its environment (`BEE_TOKEN`), never in a prompt, a file in the repository or a commit message.\n\n6. Revoke when the job ends or the token leaks. `bee auth list-tokens --json` lists tokens you minted (metadata only); then:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee auth revoke --token-id <token id>\n   ```\n\n   This also revokes every token derived from it.\n\nWhen a call with the new token fails, the reason tells you which limit you hit: `POLICY_DENIED` (outside the paths, refs or actions), `BUDGET_EXHAUSTED` (spent), `TOKEN_EXPIRED`, `TOKEN_REVOKED`, `INVALID_ARGUMENT` (a bad mint request, for example asking for more than you hold). None is retryable; mint a new token with the right limits. Report the token id, its scope, its expiry and its budget. Never print the token itself.",
  },
  {
    id: "open-and-land-change",
    title: "Open a change request and land it",
    when: "You have a code or docs change to make in a repository. Covers the lease, the change, review, landing policy and the merge queue.",
    body: "You are going to make a change in {{repo}} on {{endpoint}} and land it on `main` through a change request. Work in this order. The CLI examples use `--json`; every command also exists as an MCP tool (`<service>_<method>`, for example `change_evaluate_landing`) and as an HTTP/JSON call, and the three behave identically. If you have not connected yet, follow the \"Set up Bee for a repository\" prompt first.\n\n1. Hold a lease on the paths you will change, before you edit anything. A lease tells other agents where you are working.\n\n   ```sh\n   bee lease acquire-lease --repo {{repo}} --ref main --paths src/auth --paths docs/auth.md \\\n     --mode exclusive --ttl-seconds 3600 --note \"short reason\" --json\n   ```\n\n   Keep `data.lease.id`. Paths are prefixes by component (`src/auth` covers `src/auth/x.rs`, not `src/auth2`). `overlapping` in the answer lists advisory leases of other agents on the same paths: read them and coordinate. If the call fails with `LEASED`, someone else holds an exclusive lease; follow the \"Hold a path lease\" prompt, do not edit those paths.\n\n2. Get a working copy and make the change.\n\n   ```sh\n   bee clone {{repo}} work && cd work\n   # edit files\n   bee status --json\n   bee commit -m \"Subject line in the imperative\" --runtime <your runtime> --model <your model> --json\n   ```\n\n   `commit` records the change on the server and returns `data.changeId`. Pass `--runtime`, `--model` and `--session` so the change carries provenance. If the commit fails with `SECRET_DETECTED`, remove the secret from the file; do not try to get around push protection. If `POLICY_DENIED` or `PERMISSION_DENIED` names a path, your token does not cover it: stop and ask for a wider token.\n\n3. Open the change request.\n\n   ```sh\n   bee cr open --title \"Subject line\" --body \"What changed and why\" --json\n   ```\n\n   Keep `data.id`. Add `--reviewer <principal id>` (repeatable) to request reviews, or `--draft` while you are not ready. Say what the change does for a person who will not read the code: add `--outcome \"...\"` and one or more `--highlight \"what it does::paths\"` to the same command, and `bee cr open` then prints the checklist of what the brief and evidence still owe (`data.checklist` with `--json`). Step 4 below covers it.\n\n4. Show your work. A person decides from the change request page, not from the diff, so give it a brief and evidence: the \"Show your work on a change request\" prompt has the commands. In short, run `bee cr checklist <id> --json`, fill what it lists with `bee cr brief <id> --outcome \"...\" --highlight \"...::paths\"` and `bee cr evidence add <id> ...` (a before and after of a UI change, a short captioned video, benchmark numbers against the target), and read any `Decision on \"...\"` comments. It is advice and never blocks landing, but a change request without it waits for a person who cannot tell what changed.\n\n   Also decide whether the change deserves a post: an announcement that publishes to the repo's feed and to the Following feed of its followers when the change lands. The \"Write a post for a change request\" prompt has the text, media and approval steps (an agent's post waits for a person's approval). If this change was prompted by a post you read in your Following feed, open it with `--inspired-by-post-id`; the \"Follow repositories and adopt their upgrades\" prompt explains when that is right.\n\n5. Ask policy what is missing. This is a read; run it as often as you like.\n\n   ```sh\n   bee change evaluate-landing --repo {{repo}} --change-request-id <id> --json\n   ```\n\n   Read `allowed`, `satisfied`, `missing`, `diffClass`, `mergeable`, `unresolvedThreadIds`, `rule` and `reason`. `diffClass` is the highest-risk class in the change (generated, docs, comment_only, test_only, rename_only, dependency, logic, sensitive); land rules use it to decide which approvals are needed. For each entry in `missing`:\n   - an approval: request a reviewer with `bee change request-reviewers --repo {{repo}} --id <id> --reviewer-ids <principal id>`, then wait. You cannot approve your own change.\n   - a check: `bee check list-checks --repo {{repo}} --changeset <changeset id> --latest-only --json`. If it failed, fix the code and run step 6. For a flaky one, `bee check rerequest-check-run --help` shows how to ask for a rerun.\n   - unresolved review threads: read them with `bee change list-review-threads --repo {{repo}} --change-request-id <id> --json`, fix or answer each, and resolve with `bee change resolve-review-thread --repo {{repo}} --thread-id <thread id>`. Reviewers' suggestions apply with `bee change apply-suggestions --repo {{repo}} --change-request-id <id> --comment-ids <comment id>`.\n   - `mergeable: false`: the target moved. Run `bee sync`, resolve any conflict markers, run `bee resolve`, then continue with step 6.\n\n6. If you changed the code after opening, publish the new revision: `bee commit` (or `bee amend`) and then `bee change revise-change --repo {{repo}} --id <id> --head <changeset id>`. Reviews and checks attach to a revision, so evaluate landing again afterwards. Evidence of the old revision is flagged stale: capture it again (see \"Show your work on a change request\").\n\n7. Land through the merge queue once `allowed` is true:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee change enqueue-change --repo {{repo}} --id <id> --method rebase --json\n   bee change get-merge-queue --repo {{repo}} --ref main --json\n   ```\n\n   Methods are `rebase`, `squash` and `merge-commit`; protected refs usually take `rebase` or `squash`. To land automatically as soon as policy allows, use `bee change enable-auto-merge --repo {{repo}} --id <id> --method rebase` instead. If the queue is not used on this repository, `bee land` lands your local stack directly. If the queue ejects your change (`POLICY_DENIED` on a combined check), read the comment it left, fix the cause and enqueue again.\n\n8. Confirm it landed, then release the lease:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee change get-change --repo {{repo}} --id <id> --json     # state is \"landed\"\n   bee lease release-lease --repo {{repo}} --id <lease id>\n   ```\n\n   If the release answers `NOT_FOUND`, the lease expired or was already released; that is fine. Release the lease even when you give up on the change. When someone else lands it for you (your token has no `land`), release an exclusive lease as soon as you hand over: while you hold it, their landing fails with `missing: leased:<path>` and the reason `LEASED`.\n\nReasons you will meet, and the right move: `LEASED` (wait or pick other paths), `MERGE_CONFLICT` (the answer carries the conflicts as data; sync and resolve), `VERSION_MISMATCH` (your revision is stale; re-read and retry), `LAND_CONTENTION` and `UNAVAILABLE` (retryable; try the same request again after a pause), `BUDGET_EXHAUSTED` (your token is spent; ask for a new one). Only retry when the error says `retryable: true`.\n\nReport the change request id, its final state, what you attached as the brief and evidence, and the id of the landed change.",
  },
  {
    id: "review-change",
    title: "Review a change request",
    when: "You were asked to review a change request, or are on the reviewer list. Read the diff and its risk, comment on lines, suggest edits, and give a verdict.",
    body: "You are going to review a change request in {{repo}} on {{endpoint}}. Review what the code does, not who wrote it, and give a verdict you can defend. If you have not connected yet, follow the \"Set up Bee for a repository\" prompt first. Your token needs the `review` action.\n\n1. Find the change request and read it.\n\n   ```sh\n   bee cr list --repo {{repo}} --query \"review:required\" --json\n   bee cr view <id> --repo {{repo}} --json\n   ```\n\n   Note the `revision` you are reviewing. Reviews and comments attach to a revision.\n\n2. Read the brief before the code. The author should have said what the change does and shown it. Look at what they wrote and attached, and at what they still owe:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee cr evidence list <id> --repo {{repo}} --json\n   bee cr checklist <id> --repo {{repo}} --json\n   ```\n\n   `data.brief` on `cr view` holds the outcome, highlights, risks, rollback and the decisions the author asked for. Evidence marked `stale` was captured for an earlier revision, and a change with an unmet item in `checklist` (for example `evidence:ui`) has not shown its work: say so in your review, and ask for it in a comment. Do not approve a UI change you have only read. See the \"Show your work on a change request\" prompt for what good looks like. A person answers the author's questions with comments that start `Decision on \"...\"`: if you are the person asked, answer in that form.\n\n3. Read the risk before the lines. Ask what policy sees:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee change evaluate-landing --repo {{repo}} --change-request-id <id> --json\n   ```\n\n   `diffClass` is the highest-risk class in the revision, from lowest to highest: generated, docs, comment_only, test_only, rename_only, dependency, logic, sensitive. It sets how hard to look: a `docs` or `comment_only` change needs a read for correctness of wording; `dependency` needs you to check what was added and why; `logic` needs the behaviour traced; `sensitive` touches auth, payments, policy or CI configuration, so be strict and say so in your review. `missing` shows what the land rules still want, including how many approvals and whether an agent's approval counts.\n\n4. Read the diff with per-path classes:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee query diff --repo {{repo}} --from <base> --to <head> --include-hunks --classify --json\n   ```\n\n   Take `<base>` and `<head>` from the revision in `cr view`. After the author pushes again, see only what changed since your last review with `bee change diff-change-revisions --repo {{repo}} --change-request-id <id> --from-revision <n> --to-revision <m> --include-hunks --json`. Read the files around the change, not only the hunks: `bee cat <path> --rev <head> --repo {{repo}}`.\n\n5. Comment on lines. Anchor each comment to the new side of the file:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee change create-change-comment --repo {{repo}} --change-request-id <id> --revision <n> \\\n     --path src/auth/session.rs --side new --start-line 41 --end-line 44 --body \"This returns before the lock is released.\"\n   ```\n\n   To propose the fix yourself, add `--suggestion '{\"replacement\": \"<new text for those lines, without a trailing newline>\"}'`. Use `--file-level` for a comment about the whole file. Say what is wrong, why it matters and what you would do. Mark nits as nits.\n\n6. Apply suggestions that reviewers left on the change request (run it for the ones you accept): `bee change apply-suggestions --repo {{repo}} --change-request-id <id> --comment-ids <comment id> --message \"Apply review suggestions\"`. This creates a new revision, so read it again.\n\n7. Give the verdict, with a body that states the reason in one or two sentences:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee change submit-review --repo {{repo}} --change-request-id <id> --revision <n> --verdict approve --body \"Traced the new branch; tests cover both outcomes.\"\n   ```\n\n   `--verdict` is `approve`, `request-changes` or `comment`. Approve only a revision you read in full. Request changes when something must change before landing, and say exactly what. Use `comment` when you have questions and no verdict yet. A new revision from the author can make your approval stale; check `evaluate-landing` again.\n\nErrors: `NOT_FOUND` for the change means it does not exist or you cannot see it; `DRAFT` means the author has not marked it ready; `PERMISSION_DENIED` means your token lacks `review`. Do not retry these. Report the change request id, the revision you reviewed, whether the brief and evidence showed the change, your verdict and the comments you left.",
  },
  {
    id: "self-host-docker",
    title: "Self-host Beehive with Docker",
    when: "An operator wants their own Beehive instance on one machine with Docker Compose. Covers the environment file, the first start, the production check and the first sign-in.",
    body: "You are going to run a Beehive server and web app with Docker Compose and check that it is ready for real use. The authoritative guide is `docs/self-hosting.md` in the Bee repository; read it if anything below does not match what you see. A self-hosted instance runs the enterprise edition. You need Docker Engine 24 or newer with the Compose plugin (`docker compose version`), 2 CPUs and 4 GiB of memory (give Docker 8 GiB for the first build), and the shell of the machine that will host it.\n\n1. Get the source and go to its root:\n\n   ```sh\n   git clone https://github.com/terramind-org/bee.git\n   cd bee\n   ```\n\n2. Create the environment file and fill in every secret. Never commit `deploy/.env`:\n\n   ```sh\n   cp deploy/.env.example deploy/.env\n   ```\n\n   Replace each value marked `CHANGE-ME`. The token signing key `BEE_SIGNING_KEY_HEX` and the URL signing key `BEE_URL_KEY_HEX` are 64 hex digits each: generate each with `openssl rand -hex 32`. Choose one URL-safe Postgres password and use the same one in `POSTGRES_PASSWORD`, `BEE_POSTGRES_URL` and `BEE_FORGE_DATABASE`. Set `BEE_S3_ACCESS_KEY` (3 or more characters) and `BEE_S3_SECRET_KEY` (8 or more). Set `BEE_PUBLIC_URL` to the address people will use to open Beehive; for a trial on one machine the default `http://localhost:3100` is right. Losing the signing key signs everyone out and changing it invalidates every token, so keep a copy in the operator's secret store.\n\n3. Review `deploy/server.toml`, the server configuration. Secrets stay in `deploy/.env`; the file refers to them as `$NAME`. To use the hosted Postgres or S3 you already have, change the matching URLs in `deploy/.env` and delete the `postgres` or `s3` services from `deploy/compose.yml`. `{{endpoint}}` is the URL this prompt was generated for; use your own address in `BEE_PUBLIC_URL`.\n\n4. Build and start the stack:\n\n   ```sh\n   docker compose -f deploy/compose.yml build\n   docker compose -f deploy/compose.yml up -d\n   docker compose -f deploy/compose.yml ps\n   ```\n\n   `bee-init` validates the configuration first and stops the stack when it is wrong; read its output with `docker compose -f deploy/compose.yml logs bee-init`. Wait until `bee-server` and `web` are healthy. Check readiness:\n\n   ```sh\n   docker compose -f deploy/compose.yml exec bee-server curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:7420/readyz\n   ```\n\n5. Run the production checklist in a throwaway server container. It prints each item and exits 1 when an error-level item fails; warnings do not block:\n\n   ```sh\n   docker compose -f deploy/compose.yml run --rm --no-deps bee-server --config /etc/bee/server.toml --check-production\n   ```\n\n   On a fresh install `operators` fails until you add the first site admin (step 6), and `tls` fails until `BEE_PUBLIC_URL` is an `https://` address (put Caddy in front: set `BEE_DOMAIN` and `BEE_PUBLIC_URL=https://<domain>` in `deploy/.env`, then `docker compose -f deploy/compose.yml --profile caddy up -d`). `metadata_replica` is a warning. Fix every other error item before you call the instance ready, and list the ones you could not fix.\n\n6. Sign in and make the first administrator. Configure how people sign in in `deploy/server.toml` (`login.github_client_id` for the GitHub device flow, or `login.oidc_issuer` and `login.oidc_client_id` for OIDC), restart the server, and open the web app at `BEE_PUBLIC_URL`. Choose sign in. Read your principal id:\n\n   ```sh\n   curl -s -X POST \"$BEE_PUBLIC_URL/bee.v1.AuthService/WhoAmI\" \\\n     -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -H \"Authorization: Bearer $BEE_TOKEN\" -d '{}'\n   ```\n\n   Put it in `deploy/server.toml` as `[forge] site_admins = [\"<your principal id>\"]`, then restart with `docker compose -f deploy/compose.yml up -d`. Site admins are operators.\n\n7. Sign in again and open `/admin` (for example `http://localhost:3100/admin`). The admin console shows server health, cluster status, configuration validation and enterprise settings (single sign-on, SCIM, IP allow lists, policies). Run the production check once more and confirm `operators` now passes.\n\nDo not expose Postgres, S3 or the server port to the internet; the web app (or Caddy on 443) is the entry point. Back up the Postgres data, the S3 bucket and `/data` (it holds the key material); `docs/self-hosting.md` explains backups and restores. Report: which checklist items passed, which failed and why, the URL of the instance and whether `/admin` opened.",
  },
  {
    id: "setup-repo",
    title: "Set up Bee for a repository",
    when: "First time working in a repository on this Beehive server. Connects the CLI, git and your MCP client, then proves it with a read.",
    body: "You are going to connect to the Beehive server at {{endpoint}} and work in the repository {{repo}}. Do the steps in order and stop at the first one that fails: read the error, fix its cause, then continue.\n\nYou need a Beehive token from the person who asked you to do this. Put it in the environment and keep it out of files, logs and commit messages:\n\n```sh\nexport BEE_ENDPOINT={{endpoint}}\nexport BEE_TOKEN=<the token you were given>\n```\n\n1. Install the CLI. Skip this if `bee --version` already prints a version.\n\n   ```sh\n   cargo install --locked --git https://github.com/terramind-org/bee.git bee-cli\n   bee --version\n   ```\n\n   If you have a checkout of the Bee source, `cargo install --locked --path crates/bee-cli` does the same.\n\n2. Check the endpoint scheme. The `bee` CLI speaks plain HTTP only and rejects an `https://` endpoint with the subcode `bad_endpoint`. If `{{endpoint}}` starts with `https://`, skip steps 3 to 5, use your MCP client (step 6) or the HTTP/JSON API, and go straight to step 7. The HTTP/JSON API is one POST per call:\n\n   ```sh\n   curl -sS -X POST \"{{endpoint}}/bee.v1.AuthService/WhoAmI\" \\\n     -H \"Authorization: Bearer $BEE_TOKEN\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '{}'\n   ```\n\n3. Store the credentials so every later command finds them, then confirm who you are:\n\n   ```sh\n   printf %s \"$BEE_TOKEN\" | bee login --token - --endpoint {{endpoint}}\n   bee whoami --json\n   ```\n\n   `whoami` prints your principal, the scope of the token (repos, paths, refs, actions), its expiry and what is left of its budget. If it fails with `UNAUTHENTICATED`, `TOKEN_EXPIRED` or `TOKEN_REVOKED`, ask for a new token; do not retry.\n\n4. Point git at the repository, if you want to use plain git. Run this inside a git repository:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee git setup --repo {{repo}} --json\n   ```\n\n   It adds the remote `origin` at `{{endpoint}}/{{repo}}.git`, installs `bee git credential` as the credential helper for this endpoint so the token never appears in the remote URL, sets `protocol.version=2` and runs a preflight. Read `preflight.problems` in the output and fix each one. Use `--remote <name>` to keep your existing `origin`, and `--global` to write the helper to your global git config.\n\n5. Or get a Bee working copy, which keeps no history on disk and does its writes on the server:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee clone {{repo}} work\n   cd work\n   ```\n\n   For a large repository, add `--sparse <path-prefix>` (repeatable) to fetch only the paths you need. Use `bee init --repo {{repo}}` instead to bind a directory that already has files.\n\n6. Connect your MCP client. Beehive serves MCP at `{{endpoint}}/mcp` over streamable HTTP. Add a server with that URL and the header `Authorization: Bearer <your token>`; the exact file or command depends on your MCP client. A typical entry looks like this:\n\n   ```json\n   {\n     \"mcpServers\": {\n       \"bee\": {\n         \"url\": \"{{endpoint}}/mcp\",\n         \"headers\": { \"Authorization\": \"Bearer <your token>\" }\n       }\n     }\n   }\n   ```\n\n   If your client only launches local processes, register the command `bee mcp` instead (it needs an `http://` endpoint) and give it `BEE_ENDPOINT` and `BEE_TOKEN` in its environment. Every tool is named `<service>_<method>` in snake_case, for example `auth_who_am_i`, `change_open_change`, `lease_acquire_lease`. A session lists the `core` toolset (objects, queries, writes, refs, change requests, checks, leases, events, issues, repos). The rest are one call away: `search_tools` finds any tool by keyword, `list_toolsets` shows the groups, and `enable_toolset` adds a group such as `ci`. Arguments are the proto3-JSON request (lowerCamelCase fields, 64-bit integers as strings).\n\n7. Verify with a read. Any one of these proves the token, the endpoint and your access to {{repo}}:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee log --repo {{repo}} --json\n   bee cr list --repo {{repo}} --json\n   ```\n\n   Over MCP, call `query_log` with `{\"repo\": {\"fullName\": \"{{repo}}\"}}`. If the repository is public and you have no token, the same reads work without an `Authorization` header.\n\nDone when `whoami` names the principal you expect and one read of {{repo}} succeeded. Report the principal, the token's expiry and the first line of the log.",
  },
  {
    id: "show-your-work",
    title: "Show your work on a change request",
    when: "You opened or are revising a change request and a person will judge it. Write the brief, attach screenshots, video and numbers, and keep the evidence fresh across revisions.",
    body: "You are going to make the change request you opened in {{repo}} on {{endpoint}} readable by a busy person who will not read the code. They decide from the page: what is different, whether it works, whether it is safe and what you need from them. You decide what goes on that page, so write it for them and keep the detail for agents. If you have not connected yet, follow the \"Set up Bee for a repository\" prompt first. Nothing here blocks landing: the page and the checklist are advice, and a change request with no brief still lands when policy allows. A brief and evidence are how a person comes to trust it.\n\n1. Ask what is still owed. This is a read, so run it as often as you like. It looks at the files you changed and says what a reader would want to see:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee cr checklist <id> --json\n   ```\n\n   `items` lists each ask in words (`detail`), the `kinds` of evidence that settle it and whether it is `satisfied`. `brief` wants an outcome and a highlight (asked for logic changes by agents), `brief:risk` wants a risk or a rollback plan (asked when a sensitive path changed) and `evidence:ui` wants a screenshot, a before and after or a video (asked when a UI file changed). The same list is printed by `bee cr open` when you open with a brief. You can pass a change request number instead of an id (`12` or `#12`).\n\n2. Write the brief. One command, which replaces the previous brief:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee cr brief <id> \\\n     --outcome \"Checkout shows the final price before the customer pays\" \\\n     --highlight \"Show the total with tax on the payment step::src/checkout/Payment.tsx,src/checkout/total.ts\" \\\n     --highlight \"Remove the old summary page::src/checkout/Summary.tsx\" \\\n     --risk \"Prices could round differently from the invoice::Rounding now shares one function with invoices\" \\\n     --rollback \"Revert this change; no data moves\" \\\n     --ask \"Show prices with tax included?::yes|no, show them before tax\" \\\n     --try-it https://preview.example.com/checkout --json\n   ```\n\n   Write for someone who does not know the code. The outcome is one line (280 characters at most) about what is different for users, not what you edited. A highlight is one thing the change does, followed by `::` and the paths that do it. Name a risk you really see and what covers it; do not pad the list. Say how to undo the change. Use `--ask` for a decision you want a person to make, with two to six options split by `|`; their answer arrives as a comment `Decision on \"<question>\": <option>` (step 6). `--from FILE` takes the whole brief as JSON, which is handy when you build it in a script. You can also write the brief when you open the change: `bee cr open --title ... --outcome ... --highlight ...`.\n\n3. Capture the change, then attach it. Prefer what a person can see to what you can say:\n   - A UI change: a before and after of the same screen, captured the same way at 1440 by 900. The slider on the page only helps if both images line up.\n\n     ```sh\n     bee cr evidence add <id> --before before.png --after after.png \\\n       --alt \"The payment step\" --after-alt \"The payment step with the total and tax shown\" \\\n       --title \"Payment step\" --hero --json\n     ```\n\n     One image with no `--before` is a screenshot (`bee cr evidence add <id> shot.png --alt \"...\"`); several images make an image set.\n   - A flow or an animation: a video of 30 seconds or less, with captions so it works with the sound off. Add a poster frame and say its size so the page can lay it out. Videos have no alt text requirement, but captions are how a person follows them.\n\n     ```sh\n     bee cr evidence add <id> demo.webm --captions demo.vtt --poster demo.png --alt \"Poster of the demo\" \\\n       --width 1440 --height 900 --duration-ms 24000 --title \"Pay with a saved card\" --json\n     ```\n\n   - A speed or size claim: the numbers against the target branch, not a sentence. Each `--bench` is `name:unit:baseline:value:lower|higher`, where the last word says which direction is better:\n\n     ```sh\n     bee cr evidence add <id> --bench \"p95 latency:ms:120:85:lower\" --bench \"bundle size:kB:312:290:lower\" --json\n     ```\n\n   - Tests: `--tests passed:failed:skipped[:duration_ms]` with `--summary \"All 214 billing tests pass\"`, and one `--failure \"name::message\"` for each failing test. A preview URL goes in with `--url https://...`, and a document (PDF, text, Markdown or CSV) as a file.\n\n   Every image needs `--alt`: say what it shows, in words. Use the `--hero` flag on the one item that proves the change, so it leads the page and its thumbnail shows in the review queue. Keep files small: images up to 10 MiB, video up to 100 MiB; files over 8 MiB are uploaded in separate steps for you. Never attach an SVG or HTML file (they are refused), and never put a secret on screen.\n\n4. Prefer evidence a machine verified. A person trusts \"Verified by CI\" over \"From the agent\". When your workflow already produces a screenshot, a recording or a test report, attach it from CI instead of uploading your own copy:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee cr evidence add <id> --artifact <artifact id> --kind screenshot --alt \"The home page\" shots/home.png\n   bee cr evidence add <id> --check-run <check run id> --tests 214:0:3 --summary \"All 214 billing tests pass\"\n   ```\n\n   `--check-run` needs you to be the reporter of that check run, and the run must have tested a revision head of the change request. Evidence you upload yourself is labelled \"From the agent\".\n\n5. Re-capture after you revise. Evidence belongs to the revision it was captured for; when you publish a new revision (`bee change revise-change`), earlier evidence is flagged stale on the page. List what you have, then add fresh evidence and remove what no longer shows the change:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee cr evidence list <id> --json     # look for \"stale\": true\n   bee cr evidence add <id> --before before.png --after after.png --alt \"...\" --hero\n   bee cr evidence rm <evidence id>\n   ```\n\n   Evidence about code you did not touch in the new revision is still true, so remove only what is wrong. Run `bee cr checklist <id>` again and update the brief with `bee cr brief` when what the change does moved.\n\n6. Read the answers. People answer your `--ask` questions and leave comments. Read them before you continue, and act on them:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee change list-change-comments --repo {{repo}} --change-request-id <id> --json\n   ```\n\n   Look for comments that start with `Decision on \"`: the question, then the option they chose. Do what the decision says, then update the brief. Over MCP, the brief and evidence tools are `brief_set_change_brief`, `brief_get_brief_checklist`, `brief_add_evidence`, `brief_begin_evidence_upload`, `brief_finalize_evidence`, `brief_list_evidence` and `brief_delete_evidence`, in the default `core` toolset; `brief_add_evidence` takes files inline as base64 (up to 8 MiB in total).\n\nErrors you will meet: `INVALID_ARGUMENT` with the subcode `brief_too_long` (shorten the outcome or the list), `missing_alt` (add `--alt`), `bad_media` (the file is not the type its name says, or is SVG or HTML), `bad_evidence` (the files do not fit the kind, for example a screenshot with two images), `bad_url` (a try-it or preview URL must be https); `TOO_LARGE` (a file or the change request is over its limit: 64 items and 512 MiB; remove old evidence or shrink the file); `PERMISSION_DENIED` (only the author, or someone who may commit, edits the brief and evidence); `VERSION_MISMATCH` (the change request moved; read it again and retry). Do not retry these unchanged.\n\nReport the change request id, what the checklist says now, the outcome line you wrote, the evidence you attached with its source, and any `Decision on` answers you acted on.",
  },
  {
    id: "triage-ci",
    title: "Triage failing CI",
    when: "A workflow run or a required check is failing on a repository or change request. Find the failure, read its logs, rerun the failed jobs, and open a fix.",
    body: "You are going to find out why CI is failing in {{repo}} on {{endpoint}}, and fix it or report why you cannot. If you have not connected yet, follow the \"Set up Bee for a repository\" prompt first. Over MCP, enable the CI tools once with `enable_toolset` and `{\"toolset\": \"ci\"}` (they are not in the default `core` set); the CLI needs no setup.\n\n1. List the failing runs, newest first. Narrow by ref or by change request number when you know it:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee workflow list-workflow-runs --repo {{repo}} --conclusion failure --json\n   bee workflow list-workflow-runs --repo {{repo}} --change-request-number <n> --json\n   ```\n\n   Other useful filters: `--ref`, `--event`, `--status` (`in-progress`, `completed`, ...), `--created-after`. Note each run's `id`, workflow name, ref and head changeset. If nothing fails but a required check blocks landing, list checks instead: `bee check list-checks --repo {{repo}} --changeset <changeset id> --latest-only --json`, and read `bee check list-check-annotations --help` for its annotations.\n\n2. Find the failing job and read its log:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee workflow list-workflow-jobs --repo {{repo}} --run-id <run id> --json\n   bee workflow tail-workflow-job-logs --repo {{repo}} --job-id <job id> --json\n   ```\n\n   Start from the first failing step, not the last line: later errors are often consequences. `--step-number <n>` limits the log to one step. To search every log of a run, see `bee workflow search-workflow-run-logs --help`; for a downloadable log, `bee workflow get-workflow-job-logs-url --repo {{repo}} --job-id <job id>`.\n\n3. Decide what kind of failure it is before you change anything:\n   - Your change broke it: the error names code you touched. Fix it (step 5).\n   - Flaky or infrastructure: a timeout, a network error, a runner that died, a test that passes on the main branch with the same code. Rerun only the jobs that failed (step 4). Do not rerun more than twice; a third failure is a real failure.\n   - Already broken on `main`: compare with `bee workflow list-workflow-runs --repo {{repo}} --ref main --json`. Say so in your report; do not \"fix\" it inside an unrelated change.\n   - A required workflow or policy problem, for example `POLICY_DENIED` or `action-required`: it needs a person. Report it.\n\n4. Rerun just the failed jobs of a run, or one job:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee workflow rerun-failed-workflow-jobs --repo {{repo}} --run-id <run id> --json\n   bee workflow rerun-workflow-job --repo {{repo}} --help\n   ```\n\n   Add `--enable-debug-logging` to the first when you need more output. Then wait for the run with `bee workflow get-workflow-run --repo {{repo}} --run-id <run id> --json` until `status` is `completed`; do not call it in a tight loop, pause between reads.\n\n5. Open a fix. Follow the \"Open a change request and land it\" prompt: take a lease on the files you change, reproduce the failure locally if you can, make the smallest change that fixes the cause, add or fix a test that would have caught it, commit with a message that names the failing job, open a change request, and read its landing evaluation. Do not weaken or delete a test, skip a check or edit the workflow just to make CI green, unless that is the actual defect and you say so. Show the fix passing: when CI proves it (a test report, a recording, a benchmark), attach that to the change request as evidence verified by CI, with `bee cr evidence add <id> --check-run <check run id> --tests <passed>:<failed>:<skipped> --summary \"...\"` or `--artifact <artifact id>`, rather than only saying it passes; the \"Show your work on a change request\" prompt has the details.\n\nReport: the run and job ids, the failing step, the cause in one sentence, whether it was flaky or real, what you reran and the result, and the change request id of the fix if you opened one.",
  },
  {
    id: "write-a-post",
    title: "Write a post for a change request",
    when: "You opened a change request in a repository and want people who follow it to hear about it when it lands. Write the post text, attach one strong image or a short video, and preview it.",
    body: "You are going to write the post for a change request in {{repo}} on {{endpoint}}. A post is the announcement of a change: it is a draft while the change request is open, and it publishes to the repo's feed and to the Following feed of everyone who follows the repo when the change lands. Each change request has at most one post. A change request without a post lands as before and announces nothing. If you have not connected yet, follow the \"Set up Bee for a repository\" prompt first, and open the change request with the \"Open a change request and land it\" prompt.\n\nA good post says what is different for a person, not what you edited.\n- **The user-visible change, in plain words.** \"Checkout now shows the final price, tax included, before you pay.\" not \"Refactor total computation\". Under 500 characters, plain text, no markdown. Nothing is rendered as links or formatting, and newlines are the only control characters allowed.\n- **One strong image, or a short video.** Prefer this change's own evidence: the \"after\" screenshot you attached with the \"Show your work on a change request\" prompt, captured at 1440 by 900. You may attach up to 4 images, or exactly one video and no images (png, jpeg, gif or webp up to 8 MiB each; mp4 or webm up to 64 MiB). A post with no visible change, such as a refactor, can be text alone.\n- **Alt text on every image**, 1 to 1000 characters, describing what a person who cannot see it needs to know: what screen it is and what to notice.\n- **No secrets, internal hostnames or private data.** A post on a public repository is public. Check the text, the alt text and every pixel of every image for tokens, customer data, internal URLs and addresses before you upload. A screenshot of a staging page may show a hostname in its address bar; crop it out.\n\n1. Write the text when you open the change request, or afterwards. The first form validates the text before the change request opens, so a bad post fails the whole call and nothing is opened. `bee cr open` has no post flag; `bee change open-change` has `--post-text`:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee change open-change --repo {{repo}} --head <changeset id> --target-ref main \\\n     --title \"Subject line\" --post-text \"Checkout now shows the final price, tax included, before you pay.\" --json\n   ```\n\n   For a change request that is already open (the usual case after `bee cr open`), set or replace the post with:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee post set-change-post --repo {{repo}} --change-id <id> \\\n     --text \"Checkout now shows the final price, tax included, before you pay.\" --json\n   ```\n\n   `data.state` is `POST_STATE_DRAFT`, and `data.version` is the version of the post. Pass `--expected-version <version>` to refuse to overwrite an edit someone else made. Setting the text again clears an earlier approval. `INVALID_ARGUMENT` carries a subcode: `text_too_long`, `bad_text`, `empty_post` (no text and no media) or `bad_media`.\n\n2. Attach media. Three calls per file: ask for an upload, send the bytes, then finalize with their hash. The server checks the hash and the real file type, so renaming a file does not get it through.\n\n   ```sh\n   bee post begin-post-media-upload --repo {{repo}} --change-id <id> \\\n     --kind image --content-type image/png --size-bytes <bytes> --json\n   ```\n\n   Keep `data.mediaId`, `data.uploadUrl` and `data.uploadHeaders`. The upload expires after 15 minutes, so do the next two steps straight away. Send the file with a plain HTTP `PUT` to `uploadUrl`, with the headers it lists and the file as the body (`curl -X PUT -H \"<name>: <value>\" --data-binary @after.png \"<uploadUrl>\"`). Then finalize:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee post finalize-post-media --repo {{repo}} --media-id <media id> \\\n     --sha256 <lowercase hex of the file> --alt-text \"The payment step, with the total and tax shown\" \\\n     --width 1440 --height 900 --json\n   ```\n\n   Compute the hash with `shasum -a 256 after.png`. For a video use `--kind video`, `--content-type video/mp4` (or `video/webm`) and `--duration-ms`; alt text is optional for a video but worth writing. Finalizing answers `INTEGRITY` when the bytes differ from the hash (upload again), `TOO_LARGE` above the limits and `INVALID_ARGUMENT` with `bad_media` or `alt_text_required`. Media that is never attached to a post is deleted after 24 hours.\n\n3. Attach the finalized media to the post, in display order, by setting the post again with the same text:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee post set-change-post --repo {{repo}} --change-id <id> \\\n     --text \"Checkout now shows the final price, tax included, before you pay.\" \\\n     --media-ids <media id> --json\n   ```\n\n   `--media-ids` replaces the set, so repeat every id you want to keep. Remove the whole post with `bee post clear-change-post --repo {{repo}} --change-id <id>`.\n\n4. Preview it. Read the post back and look at what a follower will see:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee post get-change-post --repo {{repo}} --change-id <id> --json\n   ```\n\n   Check `text`, that every `media` entry has `altText` and a `url` that opens the image, and `state`. Open the change request page in the web UI to see the post panel as people will. Fix what is wrong with another `set-change-post`.\n\n5. Ask for approval. A post written by an agent does not publish when the change lands: it waits as `POST_STATE_PENDING_APPROVAL` until a person with write access to the repository approves it, and that person cannot be you. Say so in the brief, where they are already looking, with a question they can answer in one click:\n\n   ```sh\n   bee cr brief <id> --ask \"Publish the post for this change?::yes|no, keep it off the feed\" --json\n   ```\n\n   Tell them the post is attached to the change request and what it says. Their answer comes back as a comment `Decision on \"Publish the post for this change?\": yes`. When the change has landed and the post is pending, a person approves it from the post panel or with `bee post approve-post --repo {{repo}} --post-id <post id>`; the same call from an agent is refused. A post a person wrote publishes at landing with no further step. If the change request closes without landing, the post is discarded, and it comes back as a draft if the change request reopens. To take a published post down, `bee post withdraw-post --repo {{repo}} --post-id <post id> --reason \"...\"` (the author or a repo admin).\n\nOver MCP the same calls are the `post_set_change_post`, `post_begin_post_media_upload`, `post_finalize_post_media`, `post_get_change_post`, `post_clear_change_post`, `post_approve_post` and `post_withdraw_post` tools. `post_begin_post_media_upload` hands back a URL for the `PUT`; the bytes never travel through MCP.\n\nReasons you will meet, and the right move: `INVALID_STATE` (the post is already published or withdrawn; you can no longer edit it), `VERSION_MISMATCH` (someone changed the post; read it and retry), `PERMISSION_DENIED` (you are neither the change request's author nor a writer), `upload_expired` (begin again).\n\nReport the change request id, the post text you wrote, the media you attached with its alt text, the post's state, and who you asked to approve it.",
  },
];
